WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.464
Dangling pointer prevention begins with ownership.

2
00:00:02.464 --> 00:00:13.140
Before optimizing pointer usage or changing storage strategy, clarify who owns each object, who may borrow it, who may store access to it, and who is

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.372
responsible for destruction.

4
00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:18.067
When ownership is clear, many lifetime decisions become simpler.

5
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.531
Use RAII to make cleanup predictable.

6
00:00:20.531 --> 00:00:22.995
Use values when ownership is simple.

7
00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:25.869
Use std::unique_ptr for exclusive dynamic ownership.

8
00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:36.134
Use std::shared_ptr only when shared ownership is truly part of the design, and use std::weak_ptr when observation should not keep an object alive.

9
00:00:36.134 --> 00:00:39.009
Keep borrowed access short, local, and explicit.

10
00:00:39.009 --> 00:00:45.989
Raw pointers, references, spans, string views, iterators, and handles are useful, but they depend on the owner.

11
00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:54.201
A borrowed value should not be stored or scheduled for later use unless the lifetime relationship is visible and tested.

12
00:00:54.201 --> 00:00:58.307
Treat containers, temporaries, callbacks, captures, and asynchronous work with care.

13
00:00:58.307 --> 00:01:00.361
Container mutation can invalidate observers.

14
00:01:00.361 --> 00:01:02.414
Views can outlive their storage.

15
00:01:02.414 --> 00:01:04.877
Captured references can outlive local variables.

16
00:01:04.877 --> 00:01:10.215
Deferred work can run after the expected owner has changed state or ended.

17
00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:14.322
Finally, reinforce the design with tests, diagnostics, review, and governance.

18
00:01:14.322 --> 00:01:20.481
Use sanitizers, debug library modes, compiler diagnostics, and lifecycle-focused tests in development and CI.

19
00:01:20.481 --> 00:01:22.534
Review lifetime-sensitive code deliberately.

20
00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:26.640
Document remaining assumptions and revisit them as the codebase evolves.
